WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.160
The Sun's vast magnetic boundary just came into sharper focus from a third of Earth's distance. This is Fascinating Frontiers, episode 186. The European Space Agency and NASA joint spacecraft moved inward to a distance of 0.3 astronomical units. It recorded the heliosphere current sheet, while the structure still held its original form. K.C.Oga-Sawara from the Southwest Research Institute directed the study of those records.

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:57.300
The near approach cut down on changes the solar wind would normally introduce at larger ranges. Loops of plasma stand out as connected to magnetic regions on the solar surface. The records supply a clearer reference point for tracking how the sheet develops farther out. If stretched flat, the boundary would span more than 60 times the Earth the Sun gap, yet remain thin enough for a spacecraft to cross in minutes. The Sun rotates once every 27 days at the equator.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:24.920
And that motion twists the field lines into a wavy three-dimensional shape. Solar Orbiter captured the pattern near its source before the wind could stretch or tangle the waves at greater distances. The sheet begins in bright loops of plasma, known as helmet streamers, that rise above zones of opposite magnetic polarity. The tilt and height of the waves can vary from one 11-year solar cycle to the next, even though the reversal process itself stays constant.

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:50.200
From the Sun's magnetic architecture, we turn to a compact stellar system that is already testing another fundamental prediction of gravity. Two white dwarfs have been found circling each other once every six minutes. This interval ranks as the shortest measured for any white dwarf pair study to date. Timing from optical observations already shows the orbit shrinking in line with energy carried off by gravitational waves.

00:01:50.200 --> 00:02:17.060
The system ranks among the earliest targets expected for future space-based gravitational wave detectors. It offers a nearby case for checking how general relativity holds under intense conditions. While this nearby system lets us watch gravity in action, astronomers elsewhere are using radio waves to map matter across billions of light years. A very weak radio signal from neutral hydrogen was picked up from a region billions of light years distant.

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:43.560
The detection came from observations reported on September 6. The emission comes from hydrogen gas that has stayed largely the same across cosmic time. Radio methods isolated the faint line against surrounding noise. The technique shows a way to chart large-scale structure across great distances. Mapping the universe at vast scales requires patience. The same is true when watching a single pulsar over more than a decade.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:03:07.000
More than 15 years of data from the park's radio telescope captured three sudden spin-ups in the young neutron star PSR J1637-4642. One of those events shifted the rotation rate by nearly three parts per million. The findings appeared in a paper posted to archive on August 20, and later accepted by the astrophysical journal letters.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:20.280
Earlier monitoring after the pulsar discovery had shown no such events, the timing measurements gave precise records of each spin change. Further observations will check whether the glitches repeat on similar intervals.

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:39.760
Neutron star interiors still hold surprises, so do the dusty birthplaces of new stars, as shown in this week's astronomy picture of the day. The astronomy picture of the day for September 7 presents the Pelican Nebula. The image displays detailed gas and dust formations along with young stars inside the nebula.

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:55.080
Professional astronomers supplied the short explanation that goes with the daily release. The release belongs to NASA's continuing series that selects one fresh image each day. Viewers reach the full archive of earlier daily images through the same NASA site.

00:03:55.080 --> 00:04:07.960
The nebula shows both emission and reflection features formed by nearby stellar activity. While we admire star forming regions from afar, Earth itself continues to show active geology visible from orbit.

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:22.080
Clouds parted over a remote South Atlantic site as Austral winter drew to a close. The cleared view revealed a bright mark of ongoing volcanic activity at Mt. Michael. NASA Earth Observatory released the satellite image.

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:30.180
The timing matches better viewing conditions around the isolated island. The bright mark stands out against ocean and land in the frame.

00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:43.420
The image adds to the seasonal record of volcano monitoring from orbit. Modern eyes in the sky give us fresh views of active worlds. Decades old launch sites on our own planet now stand as quiet monuments.

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:54.780
The article reviews the present condition of launch complex 34 at Cape Canaveral. The pad supported early Saturn rocket tests before remaining in place after the Apollo program.

00:04:55.440 --> 00:05:06.740
Florida Tech issued the historical and visual account. The complex keeps physical traces from its active years in the 1960s. The account describes the structure still visible at the site.

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:15.420
The piece sets the location inside the larger story of American launch facilities. Abandoned pads mark where our reach into space began.

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:22.020
Four centuries earlier, the first telescopic comet observation marked where that reach first extended outward.

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:32.420
Johannes Kepler recorded comet C over 1618 Q1 through a small telescope from Lins, Austria on September 6, 1618.

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:40.380
The autumn of 1618 brought three comets visible without aid, and this one was first noted over Hungary in late August.

00:05:41.080 --> 00:05:50.340
The sighting counts as the earliest comet observation made with a telescope on record. Kepler tracked the comet's position and motion for several weeks after the first sighting.

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:59.600
The comet stayed visible to the naked eye for a long stretch before fading. Historical records place the event as the start of telescopic comet studies.

00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:06.280
The white dwarf binary in a six minute orbit gives a rare case for testing predictions about gravitational waves.

00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:20.060
Its extreme closeness means the two stars already spiral inward from energy loss to the waves. Optical data can follow the orbital decay over years and supply a direct check on theory before space detectors reach full sensitivity.

00:06:20.060 --> 00:06:28.520
The pair ranks among the shortest period binaries known and stands ready as an early target once instruments reach the needed sensitivity.

00:06:29.140 --> 00:06:35.260
One remaining question is whether similar pairs exist in even tighter orbits that have so far gone undetected.

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:40.940
Next time we will be watching for updates on whether the pulsar continues its pattern of glitches.

